  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting the Centre for Community Health Research and Development (CCRD), to provide HIV and tuberculosis prevention and treatment programs in Laos and Vietnam. In Vietnam, community health care worker training includes identifying multiple and intersecting barriers that make it challenging for individuals to access health services, and the role of local community and family in addressing these barriers. In the mountainous providence of Dien Bien in Vietnam, a young woman of the Thai ethnic minority group, has given consent to share her experience. She lives with her elderly parents in Dien Bien Phu City, has no income, and has a hearing impairment so she was unaware of public health messages on HIV symptoms and prevention. A trained community health worker identified Ms Nhung&#x2019;s situation through local outreach networks. The worker through a relative built trust with the family and was able to explain the risks, testing procedures and the entitlements to treatment provided by the Government. As a result, Ms Nhung was supported by her family to be tested for HIV and on receiving a positive diagnosis she was immediately linked to the local treatment clinic for Anti-Retroviral treatment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia sets aside ten per cent of its contribution to the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis, and Malaria to deliver additional Global Fund aligned activities in the Indo Pacific Region. This support is helping to build health system capacity across many Southeast Asian and Pacific countries to provide quality, accessible and affordable services. The Vanuatu National Malaria Control Program receives the support of a long-term WHO malaria technical officer to deliver malaria elimination planning, vector control including scaling up and maintaining insecticide-treated net coverage, vector and insecticide resistance surveillance, human resources capacity development, program planning and operational research. In PNG, Fiji, the Philippines, Cambodia, and Indonesia, UNAIDS and Health Equity Matters (HEM), are helping to deliver gender-responsive and stigma-free policy and legal environments to strengthen evidence-based decision-making. The Kirby Institute is also working with PNG to advance the elimination of mother-to-child transmission of HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B. In Vietnam and Laos, the Centre for Community Health Research and Development is implementing community-based TB and HIV projects to improve the uptake, quality and monitoring of community-led HIV/AIDS and TB service delivery.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under the Global Fund Set Aide program Australia is supporting UNAIDS and Health Equity Matters (HEM) to deliver the Indo-Pacific HIV Partnership to build capacity in Cambodia, Indonesia, Fiji, Papua New Guinea and the Philippines to promote the adoption and scale-up of innovative service delivery models focusing on pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) and HIV Self-testing. In Cambodia key components included the roll out of pop-up self-testing events, the development of standard operating procedures and a training curriculum for service providers to implement two new HIV prophylaxis measures: a long-acting injectable (cabotegravir, CAB-LA) and a self-administered vaginal ring which slowly releases antiretrovirals to reduce the risk of HIV transmission (Dapivirine Ring, DVR). UNAIDS is scaling up use of PrEP and piloting community-based point of care delivery in Indonesia. Early work in Fiji includes conducting feasibility assessments and developing standard operating procedures for introduction of new tools &#x2013; such as PrEP to prevent HIV infection.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
